From: Austin Schuh <austin.linux@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2026 16:40:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Leave out the Clang nullability annotations

Clang rejects _Nullable, _Nonnull and _Null_unspecified under
-Wnullability-extension, which fails any build that includes these
headers with -Werror. The annotations carry no behavior, so define them
only when a build asks for them.
---
 absl/base/nullability.h | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/absl/base/nullability.h b/absl/base/nullability.h
--- a/absl/base/nullability.h
+++ b/absl/base/nullability.h
@@ -223,8 +223,11 @@
 // No-op except for being a human readable signal.
 #define ABSL_POINTERS_DEFAULT_NONNULL

-#if defined(__clang__) && !defined(__OBJC__) && \
-    ABSL_HAVE_FEATURE(nullability_on_classes)
+// The nullability specifiers below are Clang extensions, which Clang warns
+// about under -Wnullability-extension. They are annotations only, so leave
+// them out unless a build asks for them.
+#if defined(ABSL_ENABLE_CLANG_NULLABILITY) && defined(__clang__) && \
+    !defined(__OBJC__) && ABSL_HAVE_FEATURE(nullability_on_classes)
 // absl_nonnull (default with `ABSL_POINTERS_DEFAULT_NONNULL`)
 //
 // The indicated pointer is never null. It is the responsibility of the provider
